		<title type="html"><![CDATA[After 15 years of people asking - Why is Bullet documentation still so very bad?]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=43120#p43120"><![CDATA[
Apologies, for the deliberately provocative subject but I doubt I'm the only person to wonder this after years of usage.<br><ul><li>Bullet is 15+ years old</li></ul><ul><li>A wiki was started, but is no longer available and seems not worthy of resurrection</li></ul><ul><li>The user manual is good but is really an overview</li></ul><ul><li>When people ask questions, they are directed to the examples</li></ul>I often have to fall back on research papers, just to understand terms such as ERP and CFM.<br><br>There should be extensive documentation by now, whether provided by the author(s) or via the user base.<br>Looking at forum threads on this topic, it does look like there's been a lot of "foot dragging" on this.<br><br>Is the lack of documentation a deliberate policy?<br><br>What do others think ?<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=11736">Octavius_Ace</a> — Sat Oct 10, 2020 8:03 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
